00:01:24.619 --> 00:01:26.439
Here’s the details of that Dreamworks deal.

00:01:26.439 --> 00:01:32.149
They signed a 4 year contract to co-produce
and distribute 4 separate animated shows.

00:01:32.149 --> 00:01:35.630
Each show will be 78 episodes just like Korra
is.

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:36.909
Remember, that’s everything from book 1
to book 4.

00:01:36.909 --> 00:01:40.299
They’re not Avatar based shows, they’ll
be new IP that Dreamworks and Studio Mir are

00:01:40.299 --> 00:01:41.900
developing together.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:45.700
The deal itself from a financial standpoint
is huge.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:49.070
Before this, usually what you’d see is a
Major Studio like Dreamworks or Nickelodeon

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:51.759
would have a series they needed animated by
an overseas studio.

00:01:51.759 --> 00:01:54.400
What happens most of the time is that the
overseas animation studios get hired as subcontractors.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:02:01.259
So they get paid a very small fee and have
no creative control over the content.

00:02:01.259 --> 00:02:05.130
Studio Mir is going to be co-producing these
4 new shows, so they’ll actually get a say

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:08.349
in what the finished product looks like.

00:02:08.349 --> 00:02:18.810
If you listened to the interview Bryke just
did, he talks about the economics and political

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:20.670
aspects of traditional animation production.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:24.540
So Studio Mir really did win the lottery,
Dreamworks or Dreamworks animation I should

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:28.400
say is a huge company badly in need of a successful
series.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:31.750
